ty  um can be helped and that's just to come  back on unfortunately but that is the  experiment of the situation I think  yeah just come back on the  experimentation issue because it's  important you make a fair point you know  primates we use a small number of  primates in an American University for  the uh  um the AstraZeneca Oxford University  vaccine process  um it wasn't the case for the Pfizer  side of things  um but the one thing we learn actually  with regards to what happened with those  vaccines is they moved human trials  really quickly the level of animal  expectation experimentation in relation  to those covid vaccines has been tiny  compared with other  non-pharmaceutical based you know  regulatory systems including for  household chemicals and pesticides and  other things and it shows actually when  the pressure is on and when government  Regulators allow this to move to human  trials quickly we can get these  medicines to the market and we can get  them into people's arms in this case and  save you know millions of lives I think  the problem is not with the use of  animals it's with the regulatory system  that forces the use of animals I think  if you speak to most large companies I  used to work in the agrochemical  industry and I used to have this debate  all the time the companies I work with  did not want to use animals and  experiments but they were forced to use  them because of the regulatory system  that was in place case and often they  would have disputes with the U.S  Regulators or european Regulators about  the need to do it so I think you know it  isn't a necessary evil that we have to  kill animals to do all these experiments  I think that the covid-19 trials and the  rapid move to get those vaccines into  people which has been miraculous and I'm  hugely grateful to everyone involved as  you are we all should be  um has shown that actually the the level  of an animal experimentation needed is  is much smaller than it has been in the  past in those processes and actually we  can get to to trial much more quickly  with human trials I think it's extremely  good that it is lessening I mean that is  an excellent we can get there I think  the government are committed to that and  I think we will get there so I think it  is a step in the right direction but  just to come back on on the pen firing  issue because I want to clarify that the  phone call because you know Mr Quentin  and the aid I spoke to Mr Quentin an  hour before Penn made that phone call  and I tore a strip off Mr Quentin  without any expletives because I was  well aware of what he was doing in  Whitewater delay getting a flight  authorization working with under the  direction of Mr Wallace and I said to  him we're having real problems here you  know unless we can get the authorization  via the ministry of Defense to get into  that airport we're not going to be able  to get a a contractor to fly in and the  number of aircraft that we have  available diminishing by the hour we  have the money to do this and I was  going from operators in Istanbul to  operators in Eastern Europe to operators  in in the United Arab Emirates and Cairo  and all of them were saying listen  you've not got us a flight number we  can't get you the plane and you can't  get you the plane it's not going to  happen so Quentin was in the firing line  he got the criticism he deserved he was  muddying the waters with ministers I've  made it very clear in a number of  interviews uh what happened with Penn  was he was exasperated he was at the end  of his tether uh his people were under  direct risk he was under at risk yes he  was under pressure so he left a very  expletive written message I think most  people in that situation would have  probably done the same quite frankly and  I think actually the release of that the  release of that tape to the Sunday Times  which had a long discussion with them  before they published it in my view was  fundamentally wrong and I've debated  with Mr Wallace and I'll continue to  about what he did on that issue can I  cut can I cut him here because we're  running out of time and fascinating  discussion we're having here but I just  entity  um to respond to William Will's point  about social media and the fact that the  content of that voicemail was penfolding  knowing very well what he could unleash  do you not think that I mean I know that  you're a keen user of Twitter yourself  do you not think that a lot of this is  whipping up mob mentality in In a  Sentimental way you know there's so many  animals who get put down because of TB  or you know so many animals that will  suffer in war zones but you know it's  social media that's really making it can  I explain to you what what would you  have done in my situation okay we'd  worked all week you know the Prime  Minister had approved the visas for the  for people to leave the 68 through  pretty Patel okay we had the whole of  defra working on getting the animals in  okay I had the money on the table from a  wealthy American investor in the United  States Spencer Haber to get the plane  that we needed to get on the airport I'd  worked on it for 10 days and I had a  political Aid suddenly in the shadows in  in Whitehall in the Minister of Defense  basically telling ministers to back off  Corsa was angry you know what I do yeah  can I just explain what we did I took to  social media okay and I made a direct  appeal to the prime minister to say  intervene after the G7 Summit I made a  little video on my phone I got over 350  000 shares on it went straight to dine  Street all the people sharing because  yes they got actually bombarded by it  and at 1 50 in the morning Mr Wallace  clearly under huge pressure from the  Prime Minister put out a tweet saying he  changed his policy 360 Degrees we could  take the animals in we could take the  people in and we could get the plane  okay then I went on Good Morning Britain  and I basically said that you know this  is a really difficult time we need to  get that plane on the ground as soon as  possible I was in tears millions of  people watching it and then Wallace  blunders in and starts to abuse me and  say that basically I was talking  nonsense I was abusing troops he was  Slaughter Charlotte Hawkins basically  pulled him up saying what are you doing  he'd been on the television and radio  the day before saying we were talking  bollocks he was making himself look  stupid he was making Downing Street  extremely uncomfortable he was trying to  undermine a policy that the whole  government had lined up to support and  ultimately ultimately he was endangering  lies because we couldn't get those  people through the gate and in the end  we didn't I hold him responsible for  that I've got to try and get them out as  others out of Afghanistan that's a  bloody difficult thing and Dominic Rabb  flying to Pakistan today and we've got  hundreds of other people that are in a  very dangerous situation I won't forgive  him for that he didn't need to delay  that flight the minute that he made that  tweet the minute the Downing Street  forced him to change I had the air  Commodore ringing me directly in the  morning via his office to say Dominic  you can have all the flight  authorization details you need just  passes on to your operators which I did  and we got it all sorted that could have  been sorted days before so that's why  that phone call was made from penfabbing  that's why I ripped a strip off Peter  Quentin that's why I went to war with  Ben Wallace in the media and that's why  ultimately he lost Dominic Dyer Wilmore  we'll have to leave it there but thank  you for joining spectator TV and last  our boarding schools emotionally  stunting boys who then grow up to ruin  the country through politics that's the  argument that the author Richard beard  makes in a new book sad little men  reviewed in the latest issue of The  Spectator by Nicholas lizard Richard is  a product of boarding school himself and  writes that he recognizes much of the  traits in prime ministers like David  Cameron and Boris Johnson from his  boarding school life  Richard beard welcome to Space Age TV  first what led you to writing this book  uh well we're in the start of the first  lockdown and I was seeing uh the way  that we were being led by our by our  government by um  uh prime minister Johnson in particular  and I was thinking how I recognized many  of his behaviors and they seemed to be  traceable back to  um to his education which  um I share although in different schools  the same form of education and I  suddenly felt it was very important to  bring these news back to everybody else  who hadn't experienced it so that the  links could be seen between  um a lot of these kind of texts and  habits which we start to take for  granted but I think do have their Source  very clearly in early life what sort of  text are we talking about here yeah  well I think the um I mean in the book  inside little men I go through the uh  various things that can be traced I  think an obvious one is the joking the  fact that that everything must be seen  to be a bit of a laugh as a way of  disarming it really so if nothing's  important nothing can can hurt you and  it's a form of self-defense which is  used  um in boarding schools in particular  um and uh it can be then extended out  into life so that anything which seems  tricky or anything which makes you feel  vulnerable can just be made into a joke  um and uh and I think that's one form of  behavior which which clearly uh runs  very deep  um in many of our Public School  politicians  so I mean self-defense is not a nation  one usually assumes uh that comes with  school was boarding school particularly  or is boarding school a particularly  traumatic experience  well I think self-defense is an  essential art to learn probably in all  schools but um in an exaggerated way in  a boarding school because you have no  one else to to do that defense for you  uh you are left there on your own  um among other boys all of whom are  trying to to hide their own sadness and  hide their own vulnerabilities and the  best form of Defense ultimately become  becomes a tag or it becomes this kind of  wall of uh  um against emotions of any kind because  if by showing emotions you can become  very very vulnerable and therefore I  think self-defense is very important in  these schools and it's one of the first  things you learn you learn for example  not to show that you're sad and homesick  as your parents drive away that becomes  a very important lesson which then can  be applied to all sorts of other  situations you know if in doubt just  close down put up a wall  I mean has it been cathartic for you to  write this book because I know that you  write that you've now moved back to your  close to your boarding school so is  there some kind of complex psychology  going on there  well I think there was but it was before  I wrote the book I I as I was here in  lockdown and I found that I'm living  half a mile  um from the school I went to which is  Radley College in Oxfordshire and I  suddenly thought well this isn't so  innocent but I mean true to my own type  my own character type  um I hadn't bothered to look into that  very deeply until we all had nothing to  do and I was allowed out you know once a  day for an hour so it seemed an obvious  place to go and to go and kind of  rummage around both now in the present  and and in terms of the past the book is  very much about the past of these  schools I went to boarding school in  1975 which was the same year as David  Cameron and Boris Johnson and I feel  sort of very  um  ready to talk about that period but not  necessarily about the schools as they  are now but about that period I do feel  that I'm an expert and I've read up on  lots of experts from the psychological  field as well it's kind of a growing  field  um and they very much say that there are  issues