sh back a bit here  because you mentioned Boris Johnson you  mentioned David Cameron and  understandably a lot of people you know  don't like them or the way that they do  things but boarding school has also  given us people like where she see anak  who are a little bit more balanced less  about joking around more about perhaps  working hard and more you've it's also  given us Churchill Orwell John le Carrie  the list goes on so are you being a bit  unfair here  well of the of the of the people you  mentioned that of the Churchill  um famously didn't like his his schools  and didn't want to claim any of his sort  of character coming from them and John  Carey very much outspoken against  um against public schooling and uh his  experience as a teacher at Eaton  um and um I think that that's uh that's  quite revealing in itself  um I'm not quite sure what you'll  pushback kind of is is say that there  are different type of people who come  out of the schools that's clearly true  um and again I would say I'm looking at  the specific period I think Rishi sunak  is a bit younger I think uh children in  these schools now will exhibit different  behaviors later on I do think there are  some things which haven't changed which  are still problematic in this  segregation from uh The Wider Community  is one of those problems which we'll  have  um an impact and will have consequences  later I mean clearly people can react in  different ways and there are people who  go on to live amazingly useful  um lives um have altruistic careers  are wonderful people  um but they do that often in spite of  their education because of course one of  the consequences is that you can then  compensate and even overcompensate to  try and  um sort of make up for that lost time  um so I'm not not I would never say that  you know that's kind of one size fits  all in the consequences it can go  exactly the other other way but it is in  spite of that education and it's  interesting then that the school